{"reference":"Luke 6","book":"Luke","chapter":6,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Blessings and Woes","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Jesus addresses people who are poor, hungry, weeping, and rejected, then sets these blessings alongside woes for those who are rich, full, laughing, and widely praised. The passage should not romanticize suffering or detach material conditions from its claims about the kingdom of God; how these dimensions relate remains a matter of interpretation.","verse_refs":["Luke 6:20-26"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_luke_006_a12cc6242ebd7c15aa04cd07"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Love of Enemies","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Jesus' command to love enemies rejects retaliation and calls for mercy that reflects God's kindness toward both the ungrateful and evil. It must not be used to require victims to remain in danger, suppress justice, or reconcile without consent and safety.","verse_refs":["Luke 6:27-36"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_luke_006_ded3c6917dbc21ff78420a5f"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}